# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8. the field commission (triads D5a)
# A flood the army is sent to, not a fight: the soldier of three years who held a crossing all night is seen by a
# general. The long shot is a commission from the ranks without the board, at 3 in every colour, each with
# grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed (core/longshot.py); a soldier is the only holder, so every single fits
# everyone who meets it. The triads at 84 are the honest other ways: the board next spring with the general's letter,
# the credit given to the section, a sergeant's stripes, a name in the general's report, the flood rescue team. Nobody
# is lost in the flood, and no child appears in it. Every colour's mean is 43.5.

== the field commission
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 20-45
holds: soldier
tenure: 3-99
per_year: 0 0 .01 .01 .005 0
drivers: fortune+.3
times: per soldier of three years or more a year: about 1 in 100 is sent with the army to a flood, a fire or a storm and does something there that a general sees (estimate); armies are called out to help after floods and fires in most years, and a commission from the ranks for one night's work is rare, a handful in a generation (estimate); about 6 lives in 1,000 become army officers (catalogue share)
likelier: a wet winter or a dry summer, a unit sent to help, a general who visits the line, years of good reports behind it
rarer: a quiet year, a posting far from any town, a general who never leaves headquarters
gap: 6-15
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work, community
horizon: week
roles: boss, friend, colleague, mentor
earth: the river floods a town, the army is sent in, and a general saw who held the footbridge all night; now the general asks to meet that soldier
tribal: the river floods the lower camp, the guard is sent down, and the war leader of the clans saw who held the crossing all night
magic: the river bursts its banks in a market town of the realm, the soldiers are sent in, and a general riding through saw who held the bridge all night
scene: The river came up through the lower town in the night, and the army was sent in. {N} spent eleven hours at the old footbridge with a rope and a torch, bringing people over one at a time: the old man from the chemist's, the woman with the dog, everyone from the terrace by the mill. Nobody was lost. A general who had come to see the flood stopped and watched, and now a staff car has come to the hall where the section is sleeping, asking for {N} by name.
scene W: {N} only did what the drill and the orders said: hold the crossing, count everyone over, leave nobody behind. It is strange to be thanked for doing one's duty.
scene U: All night {N} kept working out the river: where it was rising, where the rope would hold, how long before the footbridge went. It held because the sums were right.
scene B: A general does not send a car for an ordinary soldier every day. {N} knows a door like this opens once, and thinks fast about what to ask for.
scene R: {N} has not slept, is still wet to the waist, and has never felt so alive. The night was the best and hardest thing {N} has ever done.
scene G: The people from the terrace keep coming to the hall with tea and dry socks. The town will remember the night for fifty years, and {N} would rather stay and help it dry out than go anywhere.
scene tribal: The river rose into the lower camp in the night, and the guard was sent down. {N} held the crossing all night with a rope and a torch, bringing the old people of the camp over one by one, and nobody was lost. In the morning the war leader of the clans, who had come down to see the flood, asked who held the crossing, and has sent for {N}.
scene magic: The river burst its banks in the market town, and the soldiers were sent in. {N} held the old bridge all night with a rope and a lantern, bringing the townsfolk over one by one, and nobody was lost. A general of the realm riding through saw it, and in the morning a messenger comes asking for {N} by name.
